# Every touch of userdict in the boot files, as (file, following token),
# each a deliberate decision that userdict is the right place. Checked by
# tests/check-userdict-reach.sh alongside the compiled side: a new touch
# and a vanished one both fail until this file says so.
#
# userdict is the program's workspace. What belongs in it is what a
# program owns or a published convention expects to find there; what
# does not is machinery state, which a program can shadow or corrupt
# where the machinery parked it. The page-device request and the output
# binding used to be parked there and now live with the graphics
# machinery and the device; these are what remain:
#
#   the bootstrap        init.ps builds .xpostsys and .internaldict in
#                        userdict because nothing else exists yet, and
#                        the lockdown seals and removes them (the /exch
#                        and //.bindscope rows are that relocation and
#                        removal, and .gstatetemplate's move to the
#                        private dictionary); DATA_DIR arrives as a
#                        boot definition and is moved among the host's
#                        settings the same way
#   the language's own   statusdict, serverdict, UserObjects and the
#                        interactive quit are userdict members by the
#                        language's published shape, and err.ps keeps
#                        the program-visible error state there while
#                        mirroring the machinery's own reference
#   job workspaces       gstate.ps and err.ps open userdict to define
#                        program-facing procedures; init.ps opens it
#                        once for the same reason
#
# QUIET is a userdict occupant with no row here because no boot file
# writes it: -q writes systemdict and a -D definition lands in userdict
# through xpost_add_definitions (see tests/userdict_reach.golden). It
# stays reachable by ordinary lookup deliberately -- job streams in the
# field execute it as a bare name (tests/corpus/ghostscript/waterfal.ps,
# `QUIET not { ... } if`), so it is a program-facing convention the
# banner's own probe honours, not machinery state to evacuate.
#
err.ps begin
err.ps /.error
gstate.ps begin
init.ps begin
init.ps /DATA_DIR
init.ps exch
init.ps /.gstatetemplate
init.ps /.internaldict
init.ps /quit
init.ps /serverdict
init.ps /statusdict
init.ps /UserObjects
init.ps /.xpostsys
init.ps //.bindscope
